ng forward how is the best way or  what would the best way be for a  researcher that wants to  leverage the canine icdc and the human  cbtc for starting a new investigation  into relevant targets and dogs  a little bit of a complicated question  but really it's how do we take advantage  of some of the infrastructure that we've  invested in  to enable um new new  uh therapeutic research yeah  so maybe i can start and others can  chime in um you know i think this is one  of the  most exciting things that's been  happening um at the nih and nci level  um you know for us like theatre  community for example or the brain tumor  community we recognize that we don't  have enough investigators  that would care about pediatric brain  tumors so  many of our efforts around  interoperability  are to drive chance discovery um  and what i try to highlight in my class  in my in my talk is that  some of these emerging platforms and  their implementation provide for that  interoperability  where you know a user who might want to  create a cohort of data between brain  tumors and canines can bring them into a  common workspace  and do an analysis or  a portal environment can bring process  results from two different studies and  support that analysis  and i think the more we as a community  start doing those types of effort the  more  sort of the mimetic nature of science  will take over and others will do it  because it will drive  that chance discovery um you know for  example roles  you know in a really phenomenal study  showing that canine  gliomas share some biology with  pediatric  brain tumors is a perfect example for  the kinds of studies we want people to  do to then bring those two together  these those two data sets harmonize the  bioinformatic approaches  and deploy them in common environments  and expand that to bladder or any other  cancer  so my hope even after today is that you  know those types of efforts  um can take shape and we're certainly  happy from our side  of